{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792186118,"nonce":"l21yOxctWd+2ATPdAAufipaUlcM=","title":"Haxe 5 coroutines on krio fibers (TestCoroutines)","message":"User decision 2026-10-16: implement `@:coroutine` on stackful fibers (krio-fiber, the library Ash uses), not by porting Haxe's CPS state-machine transform (src/coro/*.ml). M:N scheduling comes later as a Dispatcher over krio-parallel.\n\nContract (upstream callUnification.ml / common.ml expand_coro_type):\n- A coroutine `f(a..):R` has the real signature `f(_hx_continuation:IContinuation\u003cAny\u003e, a..):SuspensionResult\u003cR\u003e`; Void becomes haxe.Unit.\n- In a coroutine context, `g(x)` omits the continuation and evaluates to R, suspending when g returns Pending.\n- Outside a coroutine context, `f(cont, ..)` is wrapped as `f(cont, ..).resolveTo(cont)` so a synchronous completion resumes cont.\n- `@:coroutine(transformed)` functions are written in expanded form already.\n- `@:coroutine(outcome = {noThrow: true})` lets a throw escape the call instead of reaching the continuation.\n- In a coroutine context, `for (v in x)` over an AsyncIterator, or an iterable whose iterator() returns one, calls hasNext as a coroutine, and that wins over @:arrayAccess.\n\nDesign:\n- Runtime: rayzor_fiber_new(closure) / resume / yield / current user data / free, on krio_fiber::Fiber. A resume swaps the thread-local exception handler stack and the shadow call stack, so a fiber's try/catch frames never interleave with its resumer's.\n- Std: rayzor.coro.Fiber with start(completion, body, noThrow):SuspensionResult\u003cAny\u003e and await(r):Dynamic, plus a FiberContinuation (IContinuation) whose resume switches into the fiber and calls completion.resume when the body finishes.\n- Compiler: an expanded `@:coroutine` body becomes `return Fiber.start(cont, function() BODY, noThrow)`. Coroutine calls in a coroutine context become `Fiber.await(g(Fiber.current(), x))`, and calls outside one get the resolveTo wrap. Coroutine\u003cF\u003e is the expanded function type.\n- Abstract @:coroutine constructors run synchronously, which is only observable if one suspends.\n\nKnown deviation: each coroutine call allocates a fiber. Same-fiber direct calls are a perf follow-up once conformance is done.","files":null}]}